/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  ammonia02_full_plant -- the PLOT PLAN of the synthesis loop (DEV.md 4c C75)

  HOW THIS FILE WAS MADE.  `bin/choupo-layout --draft` wrote a proposal: the
  three vessels (the only items whose sizers publish a diameter and a height)
  in flow order on one line, and the four other items listed for the author
  to place.  It was then moved by hand into the arrangement below: the two
  compressors in a compressor row to the south-west, the converter with its
  feed-effluent exchanger, the water cooler with the separator, the let-down
  drum, and a control room as a NON-PROCESS item.  The product store's seven
  bullets (DEV.md 4c C92) were placed by hand when they were sized, in a tank
  farm to the south-east, and the FENCE (C93) was declared round the plot.

  WHAT IS THE ENGINE'S AND WHAT IS OURS.  Every vessel's diameter and height
  (or length) is its sizer's own (design/<unit>/vessel...); every position below,
  every declared footprint, every separation distance, the wind and every
  zone extent are AUTHOR-SET ASSUMPTIONS FOR THIS EXERCISE -- round numbers
  chosen to make the check visible, not values taken from any spacing or
  hazardous-area standard.  Choupo ships no such table: the published ones
  (CCPS, GAP 2.5.2, Mecklenburgh; IEC 60079-10-1, EI 15, API RP 505) grant no
  redistribution, and inventing a citation for a round number would turn
  "unsourced" into "falsely sourced".

  ONE PAIR IS DELIBERATELY TOO CLOSE.  The let-down drum stands 1.9 m from
  the separator, edge to edge, against the 4 m this file declares between
  two vessels: the run ANNOUNCES it (stderr, the caveat block, the result
  JSON) rather than refusing, because a short distance is a layout a student
  must see and defend, while an overlap is not a layout at all.  Move
  `letdown` to x 62 m and the announcement goes away.

  THE SITE (DEV.md 4c C90, C93, C96).  The plant is laid out in blocks, each
  bounded by roads: the PROCESS block (the loop, inside the ring road), the
  TANK FARM to the south-east, the ADMINISTRATION AND LOGISTICS zone to the
  south and south-west (offices, canteen, control room, car park, the truck
  yard with its warehouse and laydown), and the FIRE-WATER corner to the
  north-west.  The prevailing wind blows from the south-west, so the people
  and the fire water are upwind of every release source.  Every position,
  footprint, road width, radius, the headroom and the access distance are
  AUTHOR-SET ASSUMPTIONS FOR THIS EXERCISE, said as such in each `source`;
  none is taken from a fire code or a road standard.  The ONE number that
  comes out of a published source is the truck's turning circle (`designVehicle`).

  WHY TRUCKS COME AT ALL.  The product leaves the tank farm through the fence
  as a pipe (`productStored`, out of the T201 bullets -- a world-scale ~1400
  t/day train ships by pipeline, rail or ship) and the makeup syngas ARRIVES
  by pipeline from the adjacent industrial park (`makeup`): no PROCESS stream
  moves by road.  Trucks still come, a few a week: the converter's catalyst
  (drums, at every recharge), chemicals and lubricants, spares and the
  contractors' equipment of a turnaround, and the waste the plant sends off
  site.  So the site has a WEIGHBRIDGE and a ONE-WAY TRUCK LOOP: in at the
  south gate, north on the entrance road, east on the main road over the
  weighbridge (weighed IN: what a supplier delivers is what is checked),
  south down the yard link, west along the yard road past the laydown area
  and the warehouse door, and back south to the gate -- no truck reverses or
  turns round anywhere.  Weighing OUT as well (a site that ships by road)
  would take a second deck on the outbound leg; this case does not need it.
  The engine walks the route against the design vehicle and announces any
  reversal, any corner tighter than the truck turns, a weighbridge or a stop
  the route misses and a route that leaves by no gate (none, as shipped).

  CARS AND PEOPLE.  The car park is the first turn after the gate barrier,
  east of the entrance road and south of the truck yard, so a private car
  never drives a process road or the truck loop; it sits inside the fence
  because this fence is the site boundary the plot prices, and before the
  process area, more than 30 m from every process item.  Its bays are
  REVERSE-IN at 90 deg: the manoeuvre is made on arrival, when there is time,
  and in an evacuation every car leaves nose first with a full view of the
  aisle.  The offices stand beside the gate, so visitors never cross the
  plant, upwind and out of every zone, held 30 m from the process by the
  separation table like the control room.  Two MUSTER POINTS, neither on the
  downwind side of the prevailing wind and on opposite sides of the site (south by the
  offices, north-west by the fire water), so whichever way the wind turns
  one of them is clear of the cloud; the engine announces a muster point or
  the fire-water pump house inside a release's zone or downwind of it.  A
  WINDSOCK beside the main road shows the wind to the control room, the
  canteen and both muster points.

  FIRE.  A second, EMERGENCY gate on the north fence gives the fire brigade
  a second way in from the north road when the south gate is blocked (the
  engine announces a site with one entrance).  The fire-water tank and its
  pump house stand in the north-west corner, upwind of the plant and held
  30 m off every process item.  NOT DRAWN, said rather than invented: the
  fire-water ring main and its hydrants (a pipe network with its own
  hydraulics; on a real site it follows the ring road), the escape routes
  inside the units, and any toxic-gas refuge in the control room.  The
  tank farm's SECONDARY CONTAINMENT is declared (DEV.md 4c C134, `bunds`
  below): an impounding area under the bullets, draining to a remote pit.

  Grammar and checks: docs/design/a-plant-layout-is-declared-and-checked.md
